3.7. Machine learning and deep learning methods
3.7.1. Classical machine learning
Support vector machines, random forests, gradient boosting ensembles, and logistic regression were the most widely applied classical approaches across 178 ML- and DL-focused studies. SVMs with radial basis function kernels demonstrated strong performance on voice data, achieving accuracy of 91.4% and AUC of 0.968 in the foundational Oxford PD study (Tsanas et al., 2010), and have been extensively validated on gait data with comparable results (Schlachetzki et al., 2017, Al-Sharman et al., 2023). Random forests (Breiman, 2001) offer the advantages of built-in feature importance ranking, robustness to outliers and missing values, and reliable performance on tabular clinical data; applied to PPMI biomarker features, they achieved AUC of 0.87–0.93 for early PD classification (Nalls et al., 2021). Gradient boosting approaches including XGBoost (Chen and Guestrin, 2016) achieve state-of-the-art performance on structured tabular data and have the added advantage of supporting SHAP-based post-hoc interpretability, enabling per-sample feature attribution that is directly useful for clinical decisions (Ren et al., 2025, Lundberg and Lee, 2017). Across 38 studies applying classical ML to PD versus healthy control (HC) classification tasks, the median AUC was 0.91 (IQR 0.87–0.95). Probabilistic neural networks (Hirschauer et al., 2015), k-nearest neighbor classifiers (Prashanth et al., 2016), and fuzzy logic systems (Abiyev and Abizade, 2016, Avci and Dogantekin, 2016) have also been evaluated with comparable or slightly lower performance. Table 8 presents a representative selection of ML and DL studies with their key metrics.

