3.8. Multimodal diagnostic approaches
3.8.1. Rationale for multimodal integration
No single biomarker captures the biological, phenotypic, and etiological complexity of PD. This fundamental constraint derives from the heterogeneity of the disease itself, in which different individuals exhibit distinct patterns of 𝛼
-syn spread, dopaminergic loss, non-dopaminergic neurodegeneration, genetic underpinning, and comorbidity. The statistical justification for multimodal integration is equally compelling: when two biomarkers provide complementary diagnostic information about different aspects of the underlying pathology, their optimal linear combination yields AUC values that strictly exceeds either modality alone (Makarious et al., 2022, Wu et al., 2024). Across 38 multimodal studies in this review, median AUC gains over the best unimodal baseline were 3.8% for early fusion, 7.2% for intermediate fusion, and 8.1% for attention-based fusion, with the largest gains occurring in the PD versus APS differentiation task and in prodromal PD detection, precisely the clinical contexts where unimodal approaches are individually the most limited.
3.8.2. Fusion architecture taxonomy
Four fusion strategies were identified and categorized across reviewed multimodal studies. Fig. 10 illustrates these strategies and their comparative AUC gains. Early fusion concatenates raw or lightly processed features from all modalities into a single feature vector prior to training a shared classifier; this approach preserves cross-modal feature correlations but requires complete data from all modalities for each subject. Intermediate fusion learns modality-specific embeddings through separate neural network branches that are merged at a representation level via concatenation, element-wise product, or cross-attention; this strategy handles missing modalities more gracefully and achieves the largest AUC gains in this review. Late fusion aggregates independent modality predictions via voting, probability averaging, or Bayesian combination; it is the most robust to missing data and the easiest to audit, but potentially discards cross-modal interaction information. Attention-based (Transformer) fusion uses multi-head cross-attention between modality-specific token sequences and represents the current state-of-the-art approach. The multimodal framework by the Frontiers Research Team (2025), integrating motor, voice, and gait token sequences through cross-attention Transformers in 312 participants, achieved accuracy of 94.2% for early-stage PD, representing an 8.1% gain compared with the best single modality.
3.8.3. Key multimodal studies
A logistics regression integrating 𝛼
-syn SAA, NfL, and DAT-SPECT in 198 prospectively recruited participants achieved sensitivity of 96% and specificity of 93%, compared to sensitivity of 88% and specificity of 90% for SAA alone, demonstrating a 8% sensitivity gain from combining three orthogonal biological dimensions (Parnetti et al., 2019). A voting ensemble combining DAT-SPECT striatal binding ratios with IMU gait parameters in 280 participants improved AUC from 0.882 for DAT alone to 0.956 for the combined model for PD versus APS discrimination (Song et al., 2022). An autoencoder-stacking ML architecture fusing MRI morphometry with SNCA-locus SNP data in the PPMI cohort (𝑛=423
) achieved AUC of 0.94, with biologically plausible features including left hemisphere frontal gyrification and right hemisphere parietal thickness has emerged as the dominant imaging contributor (Yang et al., 2025). The GenoML framework, an automated ML approach integrating clinical, genetic, and imaging PPMI data in 1014 participants, achieved AUC of 0.931 with hold-out validation, demonstrating that even automated pipelines without human feature engineering can achieve near-expert performance for multimodal data (Nalls et al., 2021). Table 11 summarizes quantitative evidence for key multimodal studies, including direct comparison with the best available unimodal baseline from the same study.
